<section class="split">
  <div>
    <p>
      Storage is one of the most important parts of computer architecture
      because it allows computers to keep programs, files, instructions, and
      data even when power is turned off. Without storage, a computer would
      not be able to preserve work, load operating systems, install applications,
      or retrieve information later.
    </p>
    <p>
      This exhibit follows the historical development of storage devices.
      The story begins with physical punched media, moves through magnetic and
      optical storage, and ends with solid-state drives and cloud storage.
      Across each generation, the same goal appears again and again: store more
      data, access it faster, make it easier to carry, and make it more reliable.
    </p>
  </div>

  <aside class="then-now-panel">
    <h3>Then vs. Now</h3>
    <div class="then-now-stack">
      <div>
        <strong>Then</strong>
        <p>
          Data was physical, bulky, and often handled by people directly.
        </p>
      </div>
      <span>↓</span>
      <div>
        <strong>Now</strong>
        <p>
          Data can be stored electronically, accessed instantly, and synced
          across devices.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </aside>
</section>
